Yes. Especially with children and people who model you closely, self-treatment becomes instruction.

If they repeatedly see you speak to yourself with contempt, accept disrespect, deny yourself rest, or treat every mistake as proof of failure, they can absorb that as a template for how a person is supposed to relate to themselves. If they see you hold standards and dignity at the same time, repair after mistakes, enjoy your own life, and refuse needless self-cruelty, that becomes a different template.

So self-love is not just inward feeling. It is demonstrated behavior:

\text{how I treat myself} \rightarrow \text{what I teach is permissible}

Not narcissism, not exemption from accountability. More like: this is how a human being under my care deserves to be treated — including me.

And there is a useful inversion in it: sometimes the easiest way to ask, “Am I treating myself well?” is, “Would I want someone I love to learn this way of living from watching me?”